<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quotations from Chairman Mao Tse-tung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1964">1964</a>–<a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Little Red Book, Quotations from Chairman Mao, Treasured Red Book</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quotations from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Chairman Mao</a> Tse-tung, known in English as the Little Red Book, is a compilation of quotations from the speeches and writings of Mao Zedong, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 1964 to 1979 and widely distributed during the Cultural Revolution. It was first compiled by an office of the PLA Daily as an ideological handbook for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a>. The definitive 1965 version held 427 quotations in 33 thematic chapters, and from August 1966 the People's Publishing House printed it for the public. From the mid-1960s through the mid-1970s it was the most printed book globally. On 12 February 1979 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a> ordered a halt to its publication.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quotations-from-chairman-mao-tse-tung.jpg" alt="Quotations from Chairman Mao Tse-tung" width="330" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scanner: Jp16103 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Little_Red_Book_First_Edition_English_Scan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
